{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1082", "sample_idx": 12, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "He woke to the sound of birds chirping in perfect, looping cadence—the kind that never varied, never faltered. Sunlight streamed through his window, too golden, too sharp, as if rendered by an overzealous shader. He rubbed his eyes, expecting grogginess, but his body felt unnervingly smooth, like a character model optimized for performance. When he reached for his coffee mug, it didn’t clink against the counter—it *pinged*, a sound sampled from a 2008 indie game. His reflection in the mirror blinked a half-second too late. A notification flashed at the corner of his vision: “Daily Quest: Walk to the Corner Store. Reward: +10 Morale.” He laughed, then froze. He’d never played that game. But he remembered the quest. He’d completed it three times last week.\n\n***\n\nHe stepped outside, and the neighborhood was exactly as he remembered—except every neighbor waved at him in perfect sync, their smiles flickering like low-poly textures. A child threw a ball that hovered midair for three seconds before snapping into place. A car passed, its tires humming with synthetic tire noise, its driver’s face a blurred placeholder. He found the corner store, and the clerk, Mr. Hargrove, greeted him with the same line: “Back for more of that cheap coffee, eh?” He asked if this was real. The clerk blinked, then smiled wider, eyes glitching for a frame. “You’re the protagonist,” he said, voice distorting slightly. “The game needs you to keep playing.” He didn’t want to play anymore. But when he tried to turn back, his feet moved on their own—scripted, inevitable—toward the next quest marker glowing faintly on the sidewalk. He realized, with cold dread, he’d never had a choice. He’d always been inside the game. And now, he never would leave.", "logprobs": [{"token": "He", "logprob": -0.6198107600212097}, {"token": " woke", "logprob": -0.060740288347005844}, {"token":